  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "A ball falls down at speed v and bounces upward at speed 0.75v. Which option gives the magnitude and direction of its change in momentum? A: 0.25mv upwards; B: 0.25mv downwards; C: 1.75mv upwards; D: 1.75mv downwards."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner feels lost, so start with the sign convention and ask one small question before calculating.",
      "content": "Let’s take upwards as positive. What sign should the ball’s initial velocity have?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Negative, because it is moving down?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has the initial sign right. Now check the final velocity sign after the bounce.",
      "content": "Exactly. After bouncing upwards at 0.75v, what is its signed final velocity?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "It is +0.75v."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner identified both signed velocities. Use Δp = m(v_f − v_i): Δp = m(0.75v − (−v)) = mv(0.75 + 1). [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"0.75 - (-1)\") -> 1.75] Arithmetic: 0.75 + 1 = 1.75. The positive sign means upwards.",
      "content": "Great! So Δp = m(0.75v − (−v)) = +1.75mv. Its magnitude is 1.75mv and its direction is upwards, so the answer is C."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "step_walkthrough",
    "final_answer": "C",
    "verify_code": "print(\"C\")",
    "techniques": [
      "Choose a positive direction",
      "Assign signs to velocities",
      "Use change in momentum: Δp = m(v_f − v_i)",
      "Use the sign of Δp to identify direction"
    ]
  }
}

