    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has the ammeter direction correct. Complete the terminal-voltage relationship. Illustrative calculator check: choose \\(\\varepsilon=12\\text{ V}\\), \\(r=2\\,\\Omega\\), and increase \\(R\\) from \\(4\\,\\Omega\\) to \\(8\\,\\Omega\\). Then \\(I=12/(4+2)=2\\text{ A}\\) and \\(I=12/(8+2)=1.2\\text{ A}\\). Terminal voltage changes from \\(12-(2)(2)=8\\text{ V}\\) to \\(12-(1.2)(2)=9.6\\text{ V}\\). [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"12/(4+2)\") -> 2] [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"12/(8+2)\") -> 1.2] [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"12-2*2\") -> 8] [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"12-1.2*2\") -> 9.6]",
      "content": "Yes—the ammeter reading decreases. The lost volts across the internal resistance, \(Ir\), also decrease, so the terminal voltage \(V=\\varepsilon-Ir\) increases. The correct combination is **A**."
